---
source: crates/instrukt-cli/tests/cases/configuration-editing.rs
expression: whole
---
company (string, required) — The company's identity on this computer: the name of its home folder `~/.instrukt/companies/<name>/`, following the grammar of a project slug.
connectors (object, optional) — The connectors the company's people reach its agent through, one named entry each; an entry under a kind's own key, such as `slack`, is of that kind.
connectors.slack (object, optional) — Each company's workspace has its own Slack app, named `agent`: Slack shows that name as the sender of every answer and in front of the status line. Create the app from the manifest below and install it to the workspace.
    The app token is generated in the app's settings as an app-level token with the scope `connections:write`; its reference goes into `app_token`.
    The bot token is issued when the app is installed to the workspace; its reference goes into `bot_token`.
    The agent hears a channel only once it has been invited to it.
    Manifest:
    display_information:
      name: agent
      description: The company's agent. Tag it in a channel or message it directly.
    features:
      app_home:
        home_tab_enabled: false
        messages_tab_enabled: true
        messages_tab_read_only_enabled: false
      bot_user:
        display_name: agent
        always_online: true
    oauth_config:
      scopes:
        bot:
          - app_mentions:read
          - chat:write
          - channels:history
          - channels:read
          - groups:history
          - groups:read
          - im:history
          - im:read
          - users:read
          - users:read.email
    settings:
      event_subscriptions:
        bot_events:
          - app_mention
          - message.im
      interactivity:
        is_enabled: false
      org_deploy_enabled: false
      socket_mode_enabled: true
      token_rotation_enabled: false
connectors.slack.admit (one of: members | admins_only, optional, default "members") — who is admitted to address this connector
connectors.slack.app_token (${NAME} secret reference, required) — the app-level token with the scope `connections:write`, generated in the Slack app's settings; an exact ${NAME} reference into the company home's secrets file, its value written with `instrukt secrets set <NAME> --company <name>`
connectors.slack.bot_token (${NAME} secret reference, required) — the bot token Slack issues when the app is installed to the workspace; an exact ${NAME} reference into the company home's secrets file, its value written with `instrukt secrets set <NAME> --company <name>`
connectors.slack.context_messages (integer, optional, default 30) — the largest number of earlier messages that accompany an addressed message: a positive whole number; 30 when omitted
connectors.slack.kind (one of: slack, optional, default "slack") — registered connector kind; defaults to the map key when omitted
connectors.slack.reply_mode (one of: turn_output | explicit, optional, default "turn_output") — whether finished turn output posts automatically or only explicit `instrukt company reply` calls post
connectors.slack.role (string, optional, default "company-agent") — role assigned to this connector's session(s); defaults to company-agent
connectors.slack.session_scope (one of: conversation | company, optional, default "conversation") — whether each conversation gets its own session or all admitted conversations share one standing session per (company, connector)
connectors.<name> (object, optional) — a further `slack` connector under its own name; set it up as `connectors.slack` shows
connectors.<name>.admit (one of: members | admins_only, optional, default "members") — who is admitted to address this connector
connectors.<name>.app_token (${NAME} secret reference, required) — the app-level token with the scope `connections:write`, generated in the Slack app's settings; an exact ${NAME} reference into the company home's secrets file, its value written with `instrukt secrets set <NAME> --company <name>`
connectors.<name>.bot_token (${NAME} secret reference, required) — the bot token Slack issues when the app is installed to the workspace; an exact ${NAME} reference into the company home's secrets file, its value written with `instrukt secrets set <NAME> --company <name>`
connectors.<name>.context_messages (integer, optional, default 30) — the largest number of earlier messages that accompany an addressed message: a positive whole number; 30 when omitted
connectors.<name>.kind (one of: slack, required, default "slack") — registered connector kind; defaults to the map key when omitted
connectors.<name>.reply_mode (one of: turn_output | explicit, optional, default "turn_output") — whether finished turn output posts automatically or only explicit `instrukt company reply` calls post
connectors.<name>.role (string, optional, default "company-agent") — role assigned to this connector's session(s); defaults to company-agent
connectors.<name>.session_scope (one of: conversation | company, optional, default "conversation") — whether each conversation gets its own session or all admitted conversations share one standing session per (company, connector)
issuer (object, optional) — Built-in lightweight per-company OIDC issuer for `sign_in.mode: issuer`.
issuer.enabled (boolean, required) — Whether this company's built-in OIDC issuer is enabled.
issuer.signing_key (${NAME} secret reference, optional) — Optional ${NAME} reference into the company home's secrets.env holding the ES256 private signing key (set with `instrukt mcp issuer init --company <name>`).
mcp (object, optional) — The MCP servers whose tools the company's agent calls, one named declaration each; a declaration here replaces the machine-wide one of the same name for this company.
mcp.<name> (object, optional) — One server, under a name following the grammar of a project slug.
mcp.<name>.credential (string, optional) — With `speaker_login: stored`, the credential key under `members.<member>.credentials`.
mcp.<name>.description (string, required) — One-line description of what this MCP server is for.
mcp.<name>.login (${NAME} secret reference, optional) — Exact ${NAME} reference into secrets.env (set with `instrukt secrets set`).
mcp.<name>.sign_in (object, optional) — Sign-in options when `speaker_login: sign-in`.
mcp.<name>.sign_in.accept_return_without_issuer (boolean, optional, default false) — Accept an OAuth return without RFC 9207 `iss` parameter.
mcp.<name>.sign_in.client (object, optional) — Company-local pre-registered OAuth client. Mutually exclusive with `client_ref`.
mcp.<name>.sign_in.client.id (${NAME} secret reference, required) — The identifier of a client registered with the authorization server beforehand; required when `client` is declared; an exact ${NAME} reference into the company home's secrets file, its value written with `instrukt secrets set <NAME> --company <name>`
mcp.<name>.sign_in.client.secret (${NAME} secret reference, optional) — That client's secret, only for a client that has one; an exact ${NAME} reference into the company home's secrets file, its value written with `instrukt secrets set <NAME> --company <name>`
mcp.<name>.sign_in.client_ref (string, optional) — Key name of a reusable host-level OAuth client declared under global `mcp_clients.<name>`. Mutually exclusive with `client`.
mcp.<name>.sign_in.identity (object, optional) — Identity lookup request for non-OpenID Provider authorization servers.
mcp.<name>.sign_in.identity.email_field (string, required) — Dot-separated JSON field path to the email address.
mcp.<name>.sign_in.identity.url (string, required) — Absolute https URL without credentials or fragment.
mcp.<name>.sign_in.mode (one of: authorization-code | issuer, optional, default "authorization-code") — `authorization-code` (interactive browser sign-in) or `issuer` (zero-click built-in per-company OIDC issuer `jwt-bearer`).
mcp.<name>.sign_in.scopes (list of string, optional) — OAuth scopes requested during sign-in.
mcp.<name>.speaker_login (one of: stored | sign-in, optional, default "stored") — How a speaker's login is obtained: `stored` (per-member credential) or `sign-in` (OAuth or built-in OIDC issuer).
mcp.<name>.url (string, required) — Remote Streamable HTTP MCP server URL (https, or loopback http).
members (object, optional) — The company's people, keyed by member: what a provider cannot supply, a person's own stored logins for the company's tools. An entry carries no role and grants nothing.
members.<name> (object, optional) — One person, under a member key unique within the company that follows the grammar of a project slug.
members.<name>.credentials (object, optional) — The person's own logins for the company's tools, keyed by the name an `mcp.<name>.credential` gives.
members.<name>.credentials.<name> (${NAME} secret reference, optional) — The person's own login for that tool, never a literal value; an exact ${NAME} reference into the company home's secrets file, its value written with `instrukt secrets set <NAME> --company <name>`
members.<name>.email (string, required) — The person's email address, by which a speaker's entry is found; two addresses differing only in case are one, and an address is listed by at most one member.
members.<name>.name (string, optional) — The person's display name; the member key when omitted.
profiles (list of string, optional) — The ordered profile names, declared in the machine's `agents.yaml`, that every session in the company's home starts on in place of the machine-wide order; an empty list is a finding, and without it the machine-wide order applies.
schema_version (integer, required) — The schema version this document is written in; `instrukt config init` writes the current one, an older document is converted and a newer one refused.
webhooks (object, optional) — Inbound event sources, keyed by event domain (`[a-z][a-z0-9_]*`); each declared domain is served at `/hooks/inbound/company/<name>/<domain>`, and a delivery's event type is `<domain>.<name>`.
webhooks.<name> (object, optional) — One event source, under its event domain: how its deliveries are verified and where each one's event name comes from.
webhooks.<name>.header (string, optional) — The HTTP header carrying the signature; required with `verify: hmac-sha256` and refused with the other schemes.
webhooks.<name>.name (string, optional) — A fixed event name for every delivery; exclusive with `name_from` and with `verify: github`.
webhooks.<name>.name_from (string, optional) — The top-level JSON field whose string value names each delivery's event, normalized to the event-name grammar; exclusive with `name` and with `verify: github`.
webhooks.<name>.previous_secret (${NAME} secret reference, optional) — The signing secret being rotated out, which verifies too until it is removed; an exact ${NAME} reference into the company home's secrets file, its value written with `instrukt secrets set <NAME> --company <name>`
webhooks.<name>.secret (${NAME} secret reference, required) — The signing secret every delivery is verified against; an exact ${NAME} reference into the company home's secrets file, its value written with `instrukt secrets set <NAME> --company <name>`
webhooks.<name>.state (list of string, optional) — Event names, without the domain, that are external state rather than occurrences; requires `subject_from`.
webhooks.<name>.subject_from (string, optional) — The dotted payload field whose nonempty string or number qualifies the source's subject for its state names.
webhooks.<name>.verify (one of: native | github | hmac-sha256, optional, default "native") — The verification scheme: `native` (`X-Hook-Signature`), `github` (`X-Hub-Signature-256` and GitHub's normalizer) or `hmac-sha256` (the declared `header`); never an unsigned fallback.
